@@ -9,6 +9,7 @@ One line per symptom in the [subsystem references](../reference/): what you see
 - package identification stops with `ValueError: subsection not found` in `pfs0_names`, or LibHac reports `ResultFsPathNotFound` opening the last file of an NSP. → [PFS0 file-name table missing its final NUL](../reference/packing.md#pfs0-file-name-table-missing-its-final-nul)
 - `no base game, only update packages: pack the base game and its update into one NUP`, even when the update carries the complete game's files (issue #1195). → [A NUP with complete standalone updates stops at "no base game"](../reference/packing.md#a-nup-with-complete-standalone-updates-stops-at-no-base-game)
 - packing a registered build from a derived profile on a multi-build game prints `[RED] this Mac app uses <ver> (<disp>), which game profile <id> was not brought up on (...): its settings, with this version's own shaders; every module is a registered build`, naming `<ver>` among the registered builds. Or, when a profile registers an update whose shaders are not in `shader_versions` and both the base and update are present, the build stays non-derived and packs `shader_versions`' shader libraries into the update instead of the update's own shaders. → [A registered update with its own shader tables is reported red or packed with another version's shaders](../reference/packing.md#a-registered-update-with-its-own-shader-tables-is-reported-red-or-packed-with-another-versions-shaders)
+- the build prints `[RED] this Mac app uses v917504 (1.8.0), which game profile 0400C3F00006E000 was not brought up on (v0 1.0.0, v917504 1.8.0): its settings, with this version's own shaders; every module is a registered build`, naming the version among the registered ones. → [A registered update packed with its own shader tables is reported as one the profile was not brought up on](../reference/packing.md#a-registered-update-packed-with-its-own-shader-tables-is-reported-as-one-the-profile-was-not-brought-up-on)
 - `[RED] a NUP packs only a game with a profile, and <title ID> has none: the survey that makes a provisional profile reads NSPs`, or the same line for a dump folder (issue #1265). A folder holding `exefs/` and `romfs/` directly, or only `updates/<version>/`, was not taken for a dump at all: the script asks for `prod.keys`, then stops with `no NSP with a readable title ID`. → [A NUP or a dump of a game with no profile stops before the survey](../reference/packing.md#a-nup-or-a-dump-of-a-game-with-no-profile-stops-before-the-survey)
 - the game reloads one asset for ever: a background loader takes about a core and the process several (Ocarina of Time: 412% CPU on the Mac, `OptimizeChangeBanc` and `ParallelDecompThread` busy all session, one model and its texture opened about 22 times a second). Or, from the NSP script: `vN: K file(s) whose bytes do not start as their names promise (a .zs file that is not a zstd stream), so the game files are damaged`, then the files. → [A damaged file in a NUP or a dump has no hash to fail](../reference/packing.md#a-damaged-file-in-a-nup-or-a-dump-has-no-hash-to-fail)
 - `pack_nup.py`, or the NSP script on a NUP, ends in a Python traceback (`RecursionError` in `tree_bytes`) instead of a `[RED]` line. → [A NUP whose folder contains itself](../reference/packing.md#a-nup-whose-folder-contains-itself)
